        <div class="section" id="intro">
            <h2><span class="section-icon">📘</span>Introduction</h2>
<div class="objectifs">
<h4>Objectives</h4>
<ul>
<li>Calculate a radial distribution feeder by backward/forward sweep and know its limits.</li>
<li>Choose an inverter control law (cos φ, Q(V), P(V)) and determine the hosting capacity of a feeder.</li>
<li>Interpret a dynamic simulation: nadir, RoCoF, grid-forming and grid-following inverters.</li>
<li>Explain the limited contribution of inverters to short circuits and its consequences for protection.</li>
<li>Formulate and read an economic dispatch optimisation (MILP), an OPF and a hybrid sizing.</li>
</ul>
<p class="prereq">Prerequisites: levels 2 to 4; notions of linear optimisation. Calculations made by the local calculation server. Duration: 20 h and a project.</p>
</div>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
            <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>
        </div>
        <div class="section" id="n5-1">
            <h2><span class="section-icon">🔌</span>5.1 Load flow of distribution networks</h2>
<p>MV and LV feeders are <b>radial</b>, with <b>high R/X</b>, often <b>unbalanced</b> (single-phase loads). Fast decoupled converges poorly on them
(level 2); the <b>backward/forward sweep</b> (Kersting) is used:</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><div class="step-content">Injections: current of each load and each DER at the current voltage (ZIP loads, inverters according to their control law).</div></li>
<li><div class="step-content">Backward sweep: current of each branch = sum of the downstream currents.</div></li>
<li><div class="step-content">Forward sweep: voltage of each node = voltage of the upstream node − Z·I of the branch.</div></li>
<li><div class="step-content">Repeat until the voltages no longer move (10⁻⁶ pu).</div></li>
</ol>
<div class="ex"><h4>Example 5.1 — IEEE 33-bus test network (Baran and Wu)</h4>
<details class="sol" open><summary>Results (NRJXpert server, file <code>exemples/ieee33_distribution.xml</code>)</summary>
<p>12.66 kV, total load 3.715 MW + 2.300 Mvar. Losses <span class="result">202.68 kW</span> (published reference: 202.67 kW); minimum voltage
<span class="result">0.9131 pu at node 18</span>; the substation supplies 3.918 MW and 2.435 Mvar; 8 iterations. The server's Newton-Raphson gives
the same voltages to within 10⁻⁶ pu.</p>
</details></div>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
            <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>
        </div>
        <div class="section" id="n5-2">
            <h2><span class="section-icon">🔸</span>5.2 Controlled inverters and hosting capacity</h2>
<p>Generation at the end of a feeder <b>raises the voltage</b> (ΔU ≈ (R·P + X·Q)/U² changes sign) and may <b>reduce then increase</b> the losses.
Inverters have standardised control laws (IEEE 1547-2018):</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Control</th><th>Principle</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
<tr><td>Imposed P, Q / fixed cos φ</td><td>Q = P·tan φ</td><td>simple; does not react to voltage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Q(V) (volt-var)</td><td>absorbs reactive power when V &gt; 1.02 pu, supplies it when V &lt; 0.98 pu</td><td>clips overvoltages, increases losses</td></tr>
<tr><td>P(V) (volt-watt)</td><td>reduces P above 1.06 pu</td><td>last resort: loss of production</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The <b>hosting capacity</b> of a node is the maximum power that can be connected without violating the constraints (V ≤ V<sub>max</sub>,
current ratings), at the worst moment: high production and <b>low load</b>.</p>
<div class="ex"><h4>Example 5.2 — PV at node 18 of the IEEE 33 network</h4>
<details class="sol" open><summary>Server results</summary>
<table>
<tr><th>PV at node 18</th><th>Losses (kW)</th><th>V<sub>min</sub> (pu)</th><th>V<sub>max</sub> (pu)</th></tr>
<tr><td>none</td><td class="num">202.7</td><td class="num">0.913 (18)</td><td class="num">1.000</td></tr>
<tr><td>0.5 MW, cos φ = 1</td><td class="num">153.4</td><td class="num">0.925 (33)</td><td class="num">1.000</td></tr>
<tr><td>1.0 MW, cos φ = 1</td><td class="num">145.8</td><td class="num">0.932 (33)</td><td class="num">1.000</td></tr>
<tr><td>2.0 MW, cos φ = 1</td><td class="num">226.7</td><td class="num">0.944</td><td class="num">1.045 (18)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2.0 MW, Q(V)</td><td class="num">249.8</td><td class="num">0.941</td><td class="num">1.033 (18)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Losses go through a minimum around 1 MW then rise again: beyond that, the PV « exports » to the substation through the whole feeder. At 2 MW,
Q(V) absorbs 0.21 Mvar and brings the overvoltage from 1.045 down to 1.033 pu, at the cost of 23 kW of additional losses. Hosting capacity at node
18 at 30 % load, V ≤ 1.05 pu: <span class="result">1.14 MW</span>.</p>
</details></div>
<div class="lab"><h4>Lab 10 — Hosting capacity in NRJXpert</h4>
<ol class="steps">
<li><div class="step-content">Draw a feeder, or open <code>exemples/hostingCapacity_ieee33.xml</code> (Help › Open a tutorial example) and calculate it in the
« Calculate a network file (XML) » window.</div></li>
<li><div class="step-content">Place a <b>photovoltaic generator</b> (Distributed resources palette) at the end of the feeder; Q(V) control.</div></li>
<li><div class="step-content"><b>Simulation › DER study settings</b>, Hosting capacity tab: bus, V<sub>max</sub> = 1.05, load during the test 30 %.</div></li>
<li><div class="step-content">Calculation <b>PV hosting capacity</b>; then <b>Distribution load flow</b> with this power: check that V<sub>max</sub> = 1.05 pu.</div></li>
<li><div class="step-content">Repeat in the middle of the feeder: is the hosting capacity larger? Why?</div></li>
</ol>
</div>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
            <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>
        </div>
        <div class="section" id="n5-3">
            <h2><span class="section-icon">📗</span>5.3 Time series</h2>
<p>A single operating point is not enough: PV production peaks at noon, consumption in the evening. The <b>Time series</b> calculation chains one
load flow per time step (24 h, one week…) with the load and generation profiles, and counts the hours out of range, the energy lost and the energy
curtailed. On the example <code>exemples/timeSeries_ieee33.xml</code>, the minimum voltage drops to 0.918 pu at 19:00 (evening peak), 8 hours are
below 0.95 pu and the losses amount to 1.78 MWh/day for 55.5 MWh supplied (3.2 %).</p>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
            <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>
        </div>
        <div class="section" id="n5-4">
            <h2><span class="section-icon">🔋</span>5.4 Microgrid dynamics</h2>
<p>A network supplied by rotating machines has <b>inertia</b>: after a loss of generation, the frequency falls slowly, which gives the governors time
to react. Swing equation: 2<i>H</i>·dω/dt = <i>P</i><sub>m</sub> − <i>P</i><sub>e</sub> − <i>D</i>(ω − 1). Inverters have no inertia of their own; two
families:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Grid-following</b> (most PV inverters): current sources synchronised by a PLL; they need a voltage reference.</li>
<li><b>Grid-forming</b> (microgrid batteries): voltage sources with P-f and Q-V droop or « virtual synchronous machine » (VSM); they can hold an
island alone and provide synthetic inertia.</li>
</ul>
<div class="eq">Indicators: frequency nadir (minimum), RoCoF (derivative, Hz/s, over 100 ms), final frequency (droop: Δf = −R·ΔP)</div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Example 5.3 — Islanded microgrid</h4>
<p>File <code>exemples/dynamics_microreseau.xml</code>: 2 MVA genset DG (synchronous machine), 1 MVA battery BESS (grid-forming), 0.5 MVA PV
(grid-following), loads 0.6 and 0.5 MW. At 0.5 s, a cloud reduces the available PV power to 20 %; at 2.0 s, 100 ms three-phase fault at node Ch2.</p>
<details class="sol" open><summary>Server results (RMS simulation, 4 s)</summary>
<p>Genset frequency: nadir <span class="result">49.79 Hz</span>, RoCoF 1.76 Hz/s, final frequency 49.83 Hz (droop: the battery and the genset share
the lost power). The PLL of the PV inverter sees larger excursions during the fault (49.42 to 50.50 Hz): it is the local measurement disturbed by the
voltage dip, not the network frequency.</p>
</details></div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Example 5.4 — STATCOM or SVC facing a load step and a fault (<code>exemples/statcom_dynamique.xml</code>)</h4>
<p>20 kV feeder (2 + j6 Ω) supplying 5 MW + 3 Mvar; at the load bus, a ± 4 Mvar STATCOM (Vref = 0.97 pu, slope 3 %). At 1 s, the reactive
load increases by 2 Mvar; at 2.5 s, a 150 ms three-phase fault at the same bus. Dynamic model: reactive current oriented by a PLL, PI voltage
regulator with slope, current limit (STATCOM) or susceptance limit (SVC). Repeat the simulation with the type « SVC ».</p>
<details class="sol" open><summary>Server results (RMS simulation, 4 s)</summary>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>STATCOM</th><th>SVC</th></tr>
<tr><td>Before the step</td><td class="num" colspan="2">0.955 pu, 1.98 Mvar (same as the load flow)</td></tr>
<tr><td>After the step (steady state)</td><td class="num" colspan="2">0.946 pu, 3.14 Mvar (= 0.97 − 0.03 × 3.14/4: on the characteristic)</td></tr>
<tr><td>During the fault</td><td class="num">0.316 pu, <span class="result">1.25 Mvar</span> (= 4 × 0.316)</td><td class="num">0.313 pu, 0.39 Mvar (= 4 × 0.313²)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>While regulating, both compensators behave alike. At their limit, the STATCOM keeps its maximum current: it supplies three times as much
reactive power as the SVC during the voltage dip.</p>
</details></div>
<h3>Induction machines: direct-on-line start and voltage dips</h3>
<p>The squirrel-cage induction motor is the most common dynamic load (pumps, fans, compressors). In a direct-on-line start it draws 5 to 7
times its rated current at a low power factor (0.2 to 0.3): the resulting voltage dip disturbs other users and may prevent the motor from
accelerating (torque ∝ V²). During a voltage dip (fault) the motor slows down; if it goes past the slip of maximum torque before the voltage
returns, it stalls and stays locked. NRJXpert uses the third-order model: transient emf <i>E</i>′ behind <i>R</i><sub>s</sub> + j<i>X</i>′
(<i>X</i>′ = <i>X</i><sub>s</sub> + <i>X</i><sub>m</sub>∥<i>X</i><sub>r</sub>), rotor flux dynamics (time constant <i>T</i>′<sub>0</sub> =
(<i>X</i><sub>r</sub> + <i>X</i><sub>m</sub>)/(ω·<i>R</i><sub>r</sub>)) and equation of motion 2<i>H</i>·d<i>s</i>/d<i>t</i> = <i>T</i><sub>l</sub> −
<i>T</i><sub>e</sub>, with a load torque <i>T</i><sub>l</sub> = <i>T</i><sub>0</sub>·(1 − <i>s</i>)<sup><i>k</i></sup>
(palette <b>Power system (load flow) › Induction machine</b>).</p>
<div class="ex"><h4>Example 5.5 — Starting a 2 MVA motor (<code>exemples/demarrage_moteur.xml</code>)</h4>
<p>A 6 kV substation (<i>Z</i><sub>sc</sub> = 0.02 + j0.36 Ω) supplies through a 0.05 + j0.6 Ω cable a 1 MW + 0.4 Mvar load and a pump
driven by a 2 MVA motor (default equivalent circuit, <i>T</i><sub>0</sub> = 0.7 pu, <i>k</i> = 2, <i>H</i> = 0.6 s), started at 0.5 s. Run the
dynamic simulation (5 s) and plot the bus voltage, the speed and the current of the motor.</p>
<details class="sol" open><summary>Server results (RMS simulation, 5 s)</summary>
<table>
<tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Bus voltage before the start</td><td class="num">5.95 kV (0.992 pu)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Minimum voltage during the start</td><td class="num"><span class="result">4.41 kV (0.734 pu)</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Starting current</td><td class="num">886 A (4.6 <i>I</i><sub>n</sub> at 0.755 pu; 6.1 <i>I</i><sub>n</sub> at 1 pu)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Time to reach 95 % of the speed</td><td class="num">1.3 s</td></tr>
<tr><td>Steady state</td><td class="num">slip 2.3 %, 1.35 MW, 0.76 Mvar, 5.80 kV</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The 26 % dip is far beyond the 10 % usually accepted for frequent starts: a starter (star-delta, soft starter) or a drive would be
needed, or a stronger network. Artificially lengthening the cable (or setting the load torque to 1 pu with <i>k</i> = 0) shows the case where
the motor no longer starts.</p>
</details></div>
<h3>Synchronous machine: voltage regulator and stabiliser (PSS)</h3>
<p>A fast, high-gain voltage regulator (AVR) holds the voltage well after a fault, but it reduces the damping of electromechanical oscillations
(about 1 Hz for a machine connected to a strong network) and may make it negative. The power system stabiliser (PSS) adds to the AVR set-point
a signal taken from the speed deviation: <i>V</i><sub>s</sub> = <i>K</i><sub>pss</sub>·[s<i>T</i><sub>w</sub>/(1 + s<i>T</i><sub>w</sub>)]·[(1 +
s<i>T</i><sub>1</sub>)/(1 + s<i>T</i><sub>2</sub>)]·Δω, the washout passing only the oscillations and the lead compensating the lag of the
excitation. Parameters: <b>Capability, AVR, PSS…</b> button of the generator (H, X'd, Xd, T'd0: DER button, « sync_gen » model).</p>
<div class="fig" id="fig-pss"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 235" width="600" role="img" aria-label="Block diagram of the voltage regulator and the PSS" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" font-family="inherit"><rect x="20" y="30" width="50" height="34" rx="5"/><text x="45" y="51.55" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">Δω</text><path d="M70 47L100 47"/><path d="M93.5526 49.7259L100 47L93.5526 44.2741" fill="currentColor"/><rect x="100" y="28" width="70" height="38" rx="5"/><text x="135" y="51.2" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">Kpss</text><path d="M170 47L200 47"/><path d="M193.553 49.7259L200 47L193.553 44.2741" fill="currentColor"/><rect x="200" y="24" width="120" height="46" rx="5"/><text x="260" y="44.35" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">washout</text><text x="260" y="57.35" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">sTw/(1+sTw)</text><path d="M320 47L350 47"/><path d="M343.553 49.7259L350 47L343.553 44.2741" fill="currentColor"/><rect x="350" y="24" width="120" height="46" rx="5"/><text x="410" y="44.35" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">lead-lag</text><text x="410" y="57.35" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">(1+sT1)/(1+sT2)</text><path d="M470 47H520V112"/><text x="526" y="82" text-anchor="start" font-size="12" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">Vs</text><rect x="400" y="20" width="190" height="56" rx="7" stroke-dasharray="4 3" stroke-opacity=".0"/><text x="300" y="14" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="currentColor" stroke="none" font-weight="bold">PSS</text><circle cx="520" cy="130" r="17"/><path d="M510 130h20M520 120v20" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="486" y="108" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">+</text><text x="552" y="126" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">+</text><path d="M520 147V175"/><text x="560" y="180" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">−</text><path d="M590 130H537"/><text x="572" y="122" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11.5" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">Vref</text><path d="M503 130H470"/><path d="M480 130L462 130"/><path d="M468.447 127.274L462 130L468.447 132.726" fill="currentColor"/><rect x="380" y="112" width="84" height="36" rx="5"/><text x="422" y="127.35" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">AVR</text><text x="422" y="140.35" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">Ka / (1+sTa)</text><path d="M380 130L350 130"/><path d="M356.447 127.274L350 130L356.447 132.726" fill="currentColor"/><rect x="260" y="112" width="90" height="36" rx="5"/><text x="305" y="127.35" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">exciter</text><text x="305" y="140.35" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">1/(1+sT'd0)</text><path d="M260 130L230 130"/><path d="M236.447 127.274L230 130L236.447 132.726" fill="currentColor"/><rect x="110" y="112" width="120" height="36" rx="5"/><text x="170" y="134.025" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11.5" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">machine + grid</text><path d="M110 130H60V64"/><path d="M60 76L60 66"/><path d="M62.7259 72.4474L60 66L57.2741 72.4474" fill="currentColor"/><text x="54" y="100" text-anchor="end" font-size="11.5" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">Δω</text><path d="M170 148V205H520V175"/><path d="M520 188L520 177"/><path d="M522.726 183.447L520 177L517.274 183.447" fill="currentColor"/><text x="345" y="220" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11.5" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">Vt</text></svg><div class="cap">The PSS reads the speed deviation Δω, keeps only the oscillations (washout filter, time constant Tw), compensates with a phase lead (T1, T2) the lag introduced by the exciter and the machine, then adds the signal Vs to the AVR set-point. It thus creates a damping torque in phase with Δω. Without it, a fast AVR (high Ka) can make the oscillation unstable.</div></div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Example 5.6 — Damping by the PSS (<code>exemples/pss_generateur.xml</code>)</h4>
<p>A 10 MVA alternator (8 MW, <i>H</i> = 3 s, <i>X</i>′<sub>d</sub> = 0.3, <i>X</i><sub>d</sub> = 1.8 pu, <i>T</i>′<sub>d0</sub> = 6 s) is connected
to the network through a 0.4 + j16 Ω line; its AVR has a gain <i>K</i><sub>a</sub> = 100. Fault at the generator bus from 1.0 to 1.1 s. Simulate
10 s with the PSS of the file (<i>K</i><sub>pss</sub> = 10, <i>T</i><sub>w</sub> = 10 s, <i>T</i><sub>1</sub> = 0.3 s, <i>T</i><sub>2</sub> = 0.05 s),
then without PSS (<i>K</i><sub>pss</sub> = 0).</p>
<details class="sol" open><summary>Server results (RMS simulation, 10 s)</summary>
<table>
<tr><th>Maximum frequency deviation</th><th>1.1 – 3 s</th><th>3 – 6 s</th><th>6 – 10 s</th></tr>
<tr><td>Without PSS</td><td class="num">0.88 Hz</td><td class="num">1.08 Hz</td><td class="num"><span class="result">1.25 Hz</span> (growing)</td></tr>
<tr><td>With PSS</td><td class="num">0.65 Hz</td><td class="num">0.37 Hz</td><td class="num"><span class="result">0.01 Hz</span></td></tr>
</table>
<p>Oscillation at 1.35 Hz. Without PSS the fast AVR makes it unstable: the amplitude grows until loss of synchronism; with the PSS it dies out
within a few seconds. The setting of <i>T</i><sub>1</sub>, <i>T</i><sub>2</sub> depends on the operating point: it must be checked (by simulation or
modal analysis) for each configuration.</p>
</details></div>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
            <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>
        </div>
        <div class="section" id="n5-5">
            <h2><span class="section-icon">⚡</span>5.5 Short circuit of inverter-based generators</h2>
<p>An inverter limits its current (1.1 to 1.5 times rated): IEC 60909-0:2016 represents it as a <b>current source</b> <i>I</i><sub>skPF</sub> in the
positive-sequence network, absent from the negative- and zero-sequence networks:</p>
<div class="eq">I"k3 = (c·U<sub>n</sub>/√3 + Σ|Z<sub>1</sub>(f,j)|·I<sub>skPF,j</sub>) / |Z₁| &nbsp;&nbsp; ip: no DC component for the inverter share</div>
<p>Consequences: in an island supplied only by inverters, the fault current is barely higher than the load current; overcurrent protections no
longer see the fault. Undervoltage or differential protections, or batteries oversized in current, are needed. NRJXpert illustrates it: 11 kV feeder
with PV, wind, battery and genset, three-phase fault → I"k = 1.656 kA of which only 76 A come from the three inverters.</p>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
            <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>
        </div>
        <div class="section" id="n5-6">
            <h2><span class="section-icon">💰</span>5.6 Economic dispatch and OPF</h2>
<p><b>Economic dispatch</b> decides, hour by hour, who produces: start-up of the gensets, charging and discharging of the batteries, purchases and sales
to the grid, load curtailment. It is a <b>mixed-integer linear programme</b> (MILP): continuous variables (powers, states of charge) and binary ones
(genset on or off, charging or discharging).</p>
<div class="eq">min Σ<sub>t</sub> [ fuel cost + start-ups + purchases − sales + battery wear + load shedding ]<br>
subject to: power balance at each hour; P<sub>min</sub>·u ≤ P ≤ P<sub>n</sub>·u; SOC<sub>t</sub> = SOC<sub>t−1</sub> + (η<sub>c</sub>P<sub>c</sub> − P<sub>d</sub>/η<sub>d</sub>)Δt; spinning reserve</div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Example 5.7 — Islanded microgrid over 24 h</h4>
<p>File <code>exemples/dispatch_microreseau_ilote.xml</code>: 1.5 MVA genset (diesel 800 F/l), 0.8 MW PV, 0.9 MWh / 0.3 MW battery.</p>
<details class="sol" open><summary>Server results</summary>
<p>Energy served 16.6 MWh; fuel 4 664 l; renewable share <span class="result">36.6 %</span>; no curtailment or load shedding; cost
<span class="result">3.75 MF/day, i.e. 226 F/kWh</span>, 99.5 % of which is fuel. The battery does 0.24 cycle: with this PV size, it mainly serves to
avoid genset start-ups.</p>
</details></div>
<p>The <b>OPF</b> (optimal power flow) adds the network constraints: voltages between V<sub>min</sub> and V<sub>max</sub>, currents, losses (DistFlow model
of Baran and Wu, linearised by tangent planes), and also controls the reactive power of the inverters. NRJXpert recalculates each hour with an AC load
flow to check: on <code>exemples/opf_microreseau.xml</code>, the difference between the OPF losses and those of the AC load flow is below 0.1 %.</p>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
            <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>
        </div>
        <div class="section" id="n5-7">
            <h2><span class="section-icon">🔹</span>5.7 Optimal sizing of a hybrid system</h2>
<p>Optimal sizing chooses both the <b>sizes</b> (kWp of PV, number of wind turbines, kWh of batteries, number of gensets, grid connection) and the
<b>operation</b> over typical days, minimising the annual cost (investments annualised by the CRF + operation), with reliability (energy not supplied)
and renewable-share constraints. Demonstration of the server calculation package (run by the administrator): 100 kW peak site in Dakar — genset alone
<b>317 F/kWh</b>; hybrid optimum 360 kWp of PV + 933 kWh of batteries + one back-up genset, <b>105 F/kWh</b>, 97 % renewable. The prices of the
demonstration are indicative: redo the study with current prices.</p>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
            <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>
        </div>
        <div class="section" id="n5-ex">
            <h2><span class="section-icon">🎯</span>Projects</h2>
<p>The projects start from the example XML files: <b>Help › Open a tutorial example</b>, then change the values in the « Calculate a network file
(XML) » window (tags <code>&lt;Pgen&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;derControl&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;H&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;Emax&gt;</code>…) and <b>Calculate on the
server</b>; « Save as… » keeps each variant.</p>
<div class="ex"><h4>Project A — Voltage profile of a rural feeder</h4>
<p>From <code>ieee33_distribution.xml</code>, place 3 PV plants of 0.5 MW at nodes 18, 25 and 33. Compare cos φ = 1, Q(V) and P(V) in 24 h time series:
hours of overvoltage, losses, energy curtailed. Propose a connection rule.</p></div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Project B — Stability of an island</h4>
<p>In <code>dynamics_microreseau.xml</code>, reduce the inertia of the genset (H) then switch the battery from « droop » to « VSM »: plot the nadir and the
RoCoF. What grid-forming battery power is needed to keep a RoCoF below 1 Hz/s?</p></div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Project C — Hybridisation of a diesel plant</h4>
<p>With <code>dispatch_microreseau_ilote.xml</code>, vary the PV power (0.4; 0.8; 1.2; 1.6 MW) and the battery capacity. Plot the cost of the kWh and the
renewable share; identify when curtailment appears. Complete with the economic analysis of level 4 (investments).</p></div>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
            <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>
        </div>
        <div class="section" id="n5-qcm">
            <h2><span class="section-icon">📋</span>Level 5 quiz</h2>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="c"><p class="q">1. Why the backward/forward sweep rather than fast decoupled in distribution?</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">it is more accurate in transmission</li><li data-k="b">it handles meshed networks better</li><li data-k="c">it converges on radial, high-R/X and unbalanced networks</li><li data-k="d">it needs no slack node</li></ol>
<div class="fb">It does not assume R ≪ X and handles the three phases; on the other hand, it requires a radial network.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="b"><p class="q">2. A large PV production at the end of a feeder, at cos φ = 1:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">lowers the voltage</li><li data-k="b">raises the voltage</li><li data-k="c">does not change the voltage</li><li data-k="d">changes the frequency</li></ol>
<div class="fb">The flow reverses: ΔU ≈ (R·P + X·Q)/U² with P negative towards the substation.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="a"><p class="q">3. The Q(V) law reduces overvoltages by:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">absorbing reactive power when the voltage is high</li><li data-k="b">supplying reactive power when the voltage is high</li><li data-k="c">reducing the frequency</li><li data-k="d">disconnecting the PV</li></ol>
<div class="fb">Q &lt; 0 lowers the voltage through X; limited effect on very resistive networks.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="d"><p class="q">4. The hosting capacity is calculated at:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">full load, without generation</li><li data-k="b">full load and full generation</li><li data-k="c">night time</li><li data-k="d">low load and high generation</li></ol>
<div class="fb">This is the situation that maximises the overvoltage and the reverse flow.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="c"><p class="q">5. A « grid-forming » inverter:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">needs a PLL to synchronise on an existing network in order to operate</li><li data-k="b">is a current source</li><li data-k="c">imposes a voltage and can hold an island alone</li><li data-k="d">does not take part in frequency control</li></ol>
<div class="fb">It is a voltage source with droop or VSM.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="b"><p class="q">6. The RoCoF is higher when:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">the inertia is large</li><li data-k="b">the inertia is low and the power loss large</li><li data-k="c">the droop is small</li><li data-k="d">the network is in overvoltage</li></ol>
<div class="fb">dω/dt = ΔP/(2H) at the first instant.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="a"><p class="q">7. In IEC 60909-0:2016, an inverter-based generator is represented in short circuit by:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">a current source in the positive-sequence network only</li><li data-k="b">an emf behind its subtransient reactance</li><li data-k="c">an infinite impedance</li><li data-k="d">an ideal voltage source</li></ol>
<div class="fb">I<sub>skPF</sub> limited by the electronics; nothing in the negative or zero sequence.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="d"><p class="q">8. The economic dispatch of a microgrid uses binary variables for:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">the voltages</li><li data-k="b">the prices</li><li data-k="c">the losses</li><li data-k="d">on/off of the gensets and the charge/discharge exclusion</li></ol>
<div class="fb">Hence a MILP problem, solved by HiGHS (SciPy).</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="b"><p class="q">9. An induction motor starts at 80 % of its rated voltage. Its starting torque is:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">80 % of its rated value</li><li data-k="b">64 % of its rated value</li><li data-k="c">51 % of its rated value</li><li data-k="d">the same value</li></ol>
<div class="fb">Torque varies as V²: 0.8² = 0.64. If the load torque is higher, the motor cannot start or it stalls (example 5.5).</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="b"><p class="q">10. What is the phase lead (T1, T2) of a power system stabiliser (PSS) for?</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">to filter the speed measurement noise</li><li data-k="b">to compensate the lag of the exciter and the machine so that the added torque is in phase with Δω</li><li data-k="c">to increase the static gain of the AVR</li><li data-k="d">to limit the field voltage</li></ol>
<div class="fb">A damping torque must be in phase with the speed deviation; the lag of the excitation chain shifts it, the phase lead brings it back (example 5.6).</div></div>
            <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>
        </div>
